  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A Noreaster will impact at least Downeast Maine\nthis weekend into Monday and possibly Tuesday, bringing gusty \nwinds, high surf, and periods of rain.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nWe are still expecting a strong Noreaster to impact our area\nfrom through the weekend and into Monday. However, we \nwill be on the far northern fringe of the Noreaster with the \ncenter staying south of Massachusetts through Sunday, and \nimpacts in Northern regions through Sunday will be much less than \nareas further south. Here is what to expect for the various \ncomponents of the storm:\n\nPeriods of Rain...Rain chances will be far greater Downeast than\nnorthern areas for the storm, with rain persisting off and on \ninto Tuesday. Short range model guidance remains resolute in the\nmore southerly track from yesterday. As a result, periods of \nrain should reach the Downeast coast by late this afternoon \nextending across Bangor and the interior Downeast through tonight.\nRainfall totals have now begun to paint a much clearer picture \nas the event approaches. Precipitation amounts between 0.75 \ninches and 1.25 inch are expected across the Bangor region and \nDowneast with localized totals up to 1.5 inches along the coast.\nMuch of this will wait until Sunday night and Monday to occur, \nbut even with these totals, we would not expect flooding. Sunday\nnight and Monday, a much weaker version of the Noreaster \nfinally lifts north and tracks into the Gulf of Maine, at which \npoint rain chances and totals finally increase over the whole \narea. Northern Maine could finally see some rain late Monday \ninto Tuesday, but it looks like it will be fairly light with \nmaybe around one quarter inch.\n\nGusty Winds...Winds from the northeast will increase throughout\ntonight, before becoming gusty overnight and through Sunday, \nthen diminishing through Monday. Winds will be stronger the \nfurther south you go but will top out around 25 mph across \nBangor and the Downeast with the aforementioned southerly track \nof the system. Isolated higher amounts along the coast cannot be\nruled out. We don't anticipate impacts from the wind other than\nmaybe a very isolated outage Downeast. \n\nHigh Surf...Seas near the shore will be fairly rough with 4-8 \nft seas, much of it as a longer period south/southeast swell. We\nare not expecting impactful coastal flooding, though waves \nbreaking on the rocks will be hazardous for any onlookers. \nConditions should be a bit more hazardous for coastal Hancock \nthan coastal Washington. Conditions slowly ease Sunday night \ninto Monday.",
